// THE SEVEN CENTRE COLLISIONS MEASURED ON REAL CUBES, as numbers and nothing else.
//
// Seven cubes (two brands: a black-body cube with a dark logo, a stickerless cube with a BLUE logo
// on its white centre) were photographed one side at a time, 2026-09-13, and read by two
// detectors: the shipped v3 and the permissive cubedet V6FT candidate. Each case below is a scan in
// which two sides' centres read as the same colour, so a sixth side could never be filed.
//
// What is here: each side's nine colour classes, per-sticker confidences, and the six per-sticker
// class scores the detector emitted, in the order the sides were photographed. What is NOT here:
// any image. The pictures stay private; these reads are enough to pin the behaviour.
//
// `truth` is the cube as it physically was. It was decided by legality over the stickers whose
// colour was not in doubt, re-checked with a wider search band, and confirmed by eye where two
// legal cubes fit — which happened twice, and once the legality-only answer was the wrong one. See
// `dev-docs/red-orange-prior-art.md` in the maintainer's notes for the measurement.
import type { ColorFace } from '../../src/ai-assemble.js';

export interface CentreCollisionCase {
  /** Which cube, which detector, and which centre was misread. */
  name: string;
  /** True when the misread centre is a white cap with a logo printed on it. */
  logo: boolean;
  /** Six captures, in the order the sides were shown. Exactly two share a centre colour. */
  captures: readonly ColorFace[];
  /** The cube as it physically was, URFDLB facelets. */
  truth: string;
}

